A||||10||Scott2M4||Bad Mobil-1 Oil?||||4||
Z||000000||Scott2M4||12-01-1999||04:14 PM||||Saw this in the rec.autos.misc newsgroup.  Don't have any details.  Maybe someone can elaborate ...<P><B>Bad Mobil-1 5W30 oil still on store shelves.  Mobil Oil is advising consumers NOT to use 5W30 oil with manufacturer codes of X19K9A3, X20J9A3, X21K9A3, X22K9A3 and X26K9A3.  The codes can be found on the bottom of the product's container.</B><P>Not sure how many of you use Mobil-1 in your cars.  I do ... sure wish I hadn't thrown away those damn containers last week!!!!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000001||fierospeeder||12-01-1999||05:05 PM||||i use mobil synthetic 10w30 for my tbird turbo, but i heard nothing about a recall.||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||12-01-1999||06:01 PM||||A recall of Mobil 1 would be BIG news...haven't heard anything about that and I sit in front of a Comdex News monitor 9 hours a day.<P>Been using Mobil 1 for over 20 years...it's the reason I've only owned 3 cars (including the '86 2m4) during that period.<P>And the 2m4 has Mobil 1 5W30 in it now.||||reg||11
Z||000003||William Federle||12-01-1999||07:33 PM||||I emailed Mobil and asked about the recall. <BR>Here's their reply:<P>Dear Mr. Federle:<P>Your email was forwarded to me for response.<P>William, a small amount of Mobil 1 5W30 motor oil was reclaimed due<BR>to a production issue that has since been resolved.  Because Mobil<BR>1 is formulated with performance reserves, the withdrawn product does<BR>not present performance problems for vehicles, it meets or exceeds<BR>all manufacturer and industry standards, and -- like all Mobil motor oils --<BR>is fully guaranteed within manufacturers' recommended oil drain<BR>guidelines as noted in each vehicle's owner's manual.  Although this<BR>limited batch could be expected to provide a superior level of<BR>performance, it was recalled to satisfy the company's own rigorous<BR>quality assurance standards.  Mobil has always been committed to<BR>providing only the highest quality product possible.<P>Thank you for contacting us and for your interest in Mobil 1 products.<P><BR>Amy Sarosdy<BR>Exxon Mobil Corporation<BR>Customer Relations<BR>Fairfax, VA<P><BR>I'm sorry that this was so long, but I figured it was important enough to share with<BR>you all.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||12-01-1999||11:08 PM||||Hey, I appreciate you bringing this up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||12-02-1999||09:10 AM||||I wonder if the folks at Mobil could provide a geographic breakdown of where this oil in question was distributed or to which retail locations.<P>Obviously few if any save the bottles or cases after changing oil, so that info would be more valuable than production codes.||||reg||11
Z||000007||Ditz88GT||12-02-1999||12:24 PM||||Maybe I should post this as a new topic but It does have to do with Moible 1.  I have heard that you should not change over to Mobile 1 or any full syn. oil on a car with a lot of miles on it.  My Fiero has 96,000 mile with only standard oil ever used.  I would love to switch to Mobile 1, but I don't want to do anything that would effect the motor.  My car runs great now, but I know the benefits of Mobile 1.  Does anyone know anything about this?||||reg||5
Z||000008||Scott2M4||12-02-1999||12:44 PM||||I'd be interested in hearing more on this.  After buying my 87SC5 with 116,000 miles on it, the very first thing I did was change over to Mobil-1 5W30 synthetic.  I'm certain the previous owner didn't use it.||||reg||4
Z||000009||ChadMan||12-02-1999||12:57 PM||||I've heard people say both things - "Yeah, switch to synthetic, it won't hurt anything" and "Don't switch on an old car - only use synthetic in a brand-new car."<P>I have a 73 Honda CB350 motorcycle that I switched to Valvoline synthetic.  After I switched, the bike developed a couple of minor oil leaks it never had before.  I asked a bike mechanic about it and he said synthetic oils have high detergent content, and might have removed some gum or varnish that was preventing the leaks.  HUH?!?||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||12-02-1999||01:38 PM||||Chadman,<P>That is somewhat true.  On older vehicles, often the internals have worn and a potential for a leak is created.  Sometimes this can be "plugged" by oil/carbon etc. residue.  Synthetic oils can sometimes break down that residue thus causing oil to leak from the vehicle.  It is not that you are doing any damage to the vehicle.  In fact you are still doing less damage.  However, you will be prone do developing more leaks.  I still say go with the fully synthetic.. unless you are dealing with a very old engine that required oil w/o detergents because of other reasons.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierospeeder||12-02-1999||01:46 PM||||I use mobil after i bought my tbird turbo 87' with 70,000 miles on it, not sure about the previous owner, but i would spend about 25 bucks for oil,5 quarts.  The synthetic is suppose to hold up to the heat and not break down, and my tbird has an oil line that lubricates the bearings, the turbo gets really hot.<BR>if you go to the <A HREF="http://www.egroups.com" TARGET=_blank>www.egroups.com</A> <BR>to the turbo   forum <BR>do a search for oil, i know a lot of members discussed oil during the recent months.  If i find anything saved ill paste it.||||reg||1
